  "worked_solution": "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,\u21d2\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,\u21d2\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1+5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5{e}^{t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1+5t & -5t \\\\ 5t & 1-5t\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
